# [[.Name]]

A game made with [Veduta](https://github.com/riftbane/veduta) [[.Engine]] for the Veduta
console: a 64-bit ARM board (Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W or Pi 5) with a 320×240 panel and a
gamepad.

## Play

Download `[[.Name]]_<version>_linux_arm64.tar.gz` from the project's GitHub Releases and
unpack it onto the console's card: the `[[.Name]]` folder it contains is the game, with its
`card.json`, and the console lists it by that. The `linux_amd64` archive runs on any other
Linux machine with a framebuffer (a text console, not a desktop session).

Home, or Select and Start together (Ctrl+Q on a keyboard), leave the game and go back to
the console's list.

While the game runs it takes the pad and the keyboard for itself, so Ctrl+C and
switching consoles do nothing until you leave with Home, Select+Start or Ctrl+Q. At a PC's text console the
console's blinking cursor may show over the game: `setterm --cursor off` hides it.

## Develop

```sh
veduta test                      # go test + every scenario in tests/scenarios
veduta simulate --scenario tests/scenarios/start.scenario.json
veduta render --scene main --out out/main.png
veduta inspect scene main
veduta doctor                    # also checks the game against the console
veduta release v0.1.0            # tag; GitHub Actions builds the console archives
```

No window opens on the machine you develop on: the game is seen through `render`,
`simulate` and `inspect`, and played on the console. The project is set up for Claude
Code: `claude` in this directory connects to the `veduta` MCP server (see `.mcp.json`) and
follows `CLAUDE.md`.

Layout: `veduta.json` (manifest), `card.json` (what the console lists), `cmd/game` (entry
point), `game/` (game logic and entity kinds), `assets/` (JSON sources for models,
textures, materials and scenes), `tests/scenarios/` (deterministic scenario tests).
